<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ancient Olympic Games</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">776 BCE–4th century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ancient Olympic Games were a religious and athletic festival held every four years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a> in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, traditionally from 776 BCE. Free Greek men competed in running, wrestling, boxing, the pentathlon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> races, and a sacred truce protected travellers to the festival. Elis organised the games through its officials, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellanodikai">Hellanodikai</a>, and they continued under Roman rule until the late 4th century CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ancient-olympic-games.jpg" alt="Ancient Olympic Games" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bgabel · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GR-olympia-palaestra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> gave the games a mythological origin, attributing them to the gods, and competing legends survived about who was responsible. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> reckoned the first Olympics to date from 776 BCE, and most later ancient historians accepted this, though the date cannot be verified. It is generally accepted that the games began some time in the eighth century BCE, and major games at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a> probably arose around 700 BCE. The victor lists show that victors' names and details were unreliable until the sixth century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the Olympic truce, announced by three runners called spondophoroi sent from Elis, armies were forbidden to enter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a>, and legal disputes and the death penalty were suspended. The truce mainly allowed athletes and visitors to travel safely and did not stop all wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Thucydides records that the Spartans were barred from the games and that violators of the truce were fined 2,000 minae for an assault on Lepreum.</p><p>After the Roman conquest the games continued but declined in popularity. In 86 BCE the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> robbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a> to finance a war. Under Augustus the games revived: Marcus Agrippa restored the damaged temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, and in 12 BCE Augustus asked King Herod of Judea to subsidise the games. A bronze plaque found in 1994 proves that an international games continued until at least 385 CE. The last recorded games were held under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> in 393, though archaeological evidence indicates that some were held afterwards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The program began with a single foot race, the stade of just over 192 metres, the only event at the first thirteen games. It grew to twenty-three contests, though no more than twenty featured at any one Olympiad. The diaulos was added in 724 BCE, wrestling and the pentathlon in 708 BCE, boxing in 688 BCE and the four-horse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> race in 680 BCE. Most events were limited to male athletes, though women could take part by entering horses in the equestrian events.</p><p>The games were also a religious festival in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, with 100 oxen sacrificed to him on the middle day. The temple of Zeus, built by the architect Libon, held a gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> statue by the sculptor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phidias">Pheidias</a>, about 13 metres tall, which was counted among the seven wonders of the ancient world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ancient Olympic Games” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Elis supervised the <b>Olympic Games</b> through officials called Hellanodikai, and its territory was regarded as sacred during the festival.</blockquote><a href="/state/elis">Elis</a></li></ul></section>
